{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"bash .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help; cat scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl | tail -3; sed -n '1,120p' figure_manifest.json; rg -n 'overview|width_decay|censoring|includegraphics|LLM Usage|qualification|validity' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"usage: scientific_evidence [-h]\n                           {list-schemas,print-spec,emit-schema,emit-bundle,verify}\n                           ...\n\nEmit and verify the canonical scientific-evidence bundle. This is a skill\nscript, not an installed CLI. Run it via bash or python.\n\npositional arguments:\n  {list-schemas,print-spec,emit-schema,emit-bundle,verify}\n    list-schemas        List canonical schema names and their fixed relative\n                        paths inside scientific-evidence/.\n    print-spec          Print the canonical markdown spec to stdout.\n    emit-schema         Write one canonical JSON Schema to stdout or --output.\n    emit-bundle         Write the fixed scientific-evidence/ bundle under\n                        --root-dir.\n    verify              Scan text artifacts, sidecars, and ledger refs under\n                        --root-dir and emit a verification report.\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"study_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 13.5,\n        \"legend\": 13.5,\n        \"ticks\": 13.5,\n        \"title\": 14\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/study_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/study_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"width_decay_outcomes\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 13.5,\n        \"legend\": 13.5,\n        \"ticks\": 13.5,\n        \"title\": 14\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 4,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/width_decay_outcomes.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/width_decay_outcomes.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        2,\n        2,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"censoring_audit\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 13.5,\n        \"legend\": 13.5,\n        \"ticks\": 13.5,\n        \"title\": 14\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/censoring_audit.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/censoring_audit.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n32:Does increasing model size change delayed generalization, or only the speed of fitting the training data? We study this distinction in full-batch ReLU multilayer perceptrons on modular addition and subtraction. A fixed learning-rate protocol crosses three hidden widths with two AdamW weight-decay settings and three initialization seeds, producing 36 completed training records. Memorization and generalization are defined by separate sustained accuracy thresholds. Within 20,000 updates, 30 records attain memorization but none attains generalization. Wider networks fit earlier without decay and fit where the narrowest networks fail with positive decay; these differences do not establish faster grokking. An audit of the reported endpoint-substituted lag shows that its positive 500-update width contrast, including a degenerate seed interval, follows from fitting times and censoring rather than observed generalization. The evidence supports a finite-horizon separation between width-dependent fitting and unobserved generalization, while leaving the eventual dependence of grokking time on model size unidentified.\n57:  \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/study_overview.png}\n59:  \\label{fig:overview}\n182:  \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/width_decay_outcomes.pdf}\n215:Figure~\\ref{fig:censoring} shows the capped statistic rather than labeling it as observed delay. Since all generalization events are unobserved, every fitted record has $\\LH=H-\\TM$. Under positive decay, width 16 receives the special non-memorization value 20,000, while width 256 receives $20,000-500=19,500$. Their paired contrast is therefore 500 updates for every seed and both operations. The supplied three-seed interval is $[500,500]$ updates because these recorded contrasts have zero sample variation. Its degeneracy reflects the endpoint rule and checkpoint-aligned fitting events, not precise knowledge of generalization time.\n223:  \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/censoring_audit.pdf}\n225:  \\label{fig:censoring}\n234:This audit also limits the working explanation. The fitting prediction is borne out within the measured grid. The proposition that wider networks suffice to reveal delayed generalization within this budget is not supported. However, the data cannot establish equal eventual generalization times across widths, nor that any of the fitted models will ever generalize. Administrative censoring preserves a finite observation window; it does not manufacture the missing event.\n245:There are no independently trained random-label controls, matched initial logit-scale interventions, norm-matched comparisons, circuit decompositions, or kernel/feature-learning measurements. Weight norm and loss are recorded, but they do not identify which of the candidate explanations in related work applies. In particular, the absence of an observed transition does not show that norm efficiency, lazy-to-rich learning, or a delayed representation change is absent. Nor can the poor endpoint accuracy identify a unique failure cause. We therefore do not attribute the result to an unmeasured circuit or diagnose an adaptivity or validity failure from performance alone.\n260:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status/value]]\n261:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome/value]]\n262:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.correct/value]]\n400:The original training environment pins \\texttt{numpy==2.1.3} and \\texttt{torch==2.5.1+cpu}. The plotting environment and exact commands are recorded in \\texttt{REPRODUCIBILITY.md}; \\texttt{analysis/plotting\\_requirements.txt} records the versions used for this manuscript. Running \\texttt{python build\\_artifacts.py} replays only analysis of stored measurements, not neural training. It produces the event table, complete observation table, diagnostic table, both quantitative figures, and \\texttt{analysis/derived\\_results.json}. The overview is supplied separately by the figure-rendering workflow. Its placeholder is not an empirical result.\n415:\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
