{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/paper-repair-tools/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport pymupdf\npaper=pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\nfor index in (7,8,9,10):\n page=paper[index]\n print('\\\\nPAGE',index+1)\n for block in page.get_text('dict')['blocks']:\n  if block['type']==0:\n   for line in block['lines']:\n    if line['dir']==(1.,0.) and line['bbox'][0]>100:\n     print(''.join(span['text'] for span in line['spans']))\nfor index,name in [(0,'first-page'),(1,'overview-page'),(7,'body-eight'),(8,'last-body')]:\n paper[index].get_pixmap(matrix=pymupdf.Matrix(1,1)).save('/tmp/paper-repair-'+name+'.png')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/paper-repair-tools/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport pymupdf\npaper=pymupdf.open('paper.pdf')\nfor index in (7,8,9,10):\n page=paper[index]\n print('\\\\nPAGE',index+1)\n for block in page.get_text('dict')['blocks']:\n  if block['type']==0:\n   for line in block['lines']:\n    if line['dir']==(1.,0.) and line['bbox'][0]>100:\n     print(''.join(span['text'] for span in line['spans']))\nfor index,name in [(0,'first-page'),(1,'overview-page'),(7,'body-eight'),(8,'last-body')]:\n paper[index].get_pixmap(matrix=pymupdf.Matrix(1,1)).save('/tmp/paper-repair-'+name+'.png')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nPAGE 8\nchange the qualitative outcome. Conversely, continued low held-out accuracy could persist without\ncontradiction. The retained trajectories give neither outcome. The capped statistic is useful only\nwhen read alongside the two event indicators; reporting it alone would erase the distinction between\nfailing to fit and fitting without generalization.\nMechanistic explanations remain open.\nThere are no independently trained random-label con-\ntrols, matched initial logit-scale interventions, norm-matched comparisons, circuit decompositions,\nor kernel/feature-learning measurements. Weight norm and loss are recorded, but they do not iden-\ntify which of the candidate explanations in related work applies. In particular, the absence of an\nobserved transition does not show that norm efficiency, lazy-to-rich learning, or a delayed represen-\ntation change is absent. Nor can the poor endpoint accuracy identify a unique failure cause. We\ntherefore do not attribute the result to an unmeasured circuit or diagnose an adaptivity or validity\nfailure from performance alone.\nUpdate budget is not compute matching.\nAll conditions receive the same number of full-batch\noptimizer updates and the same checkpoint schedule, making the event comparison interpretable\nin update units. Equal updates do not imply equal arithmetic work or equal wall-clock cost across\nwidths. The retained timing fields summarize seed-level evaluations rather than isolated, hardware-\ncontrolled per-width throughput. Consequently, earlier fitting in update units is not a measured\nspeedup per CPU second or a resource-efficiency claim. The study also fixes one learning-rate\nrecipe instead of tuning a separate optimum for each width. Its negative generalization result applies\nto that shared recipe, not to the best achievable performance of every model size.\nCoverage and uncertainty.\nThe empirical scope is one modulus, one input representation, one\nhidden-layer family, three widths, two decay values, three initialization seeds, and a fixed split\nper operation. The operations are closely related, not a representative distribution of algorithmic\ntasks. The small seed count limits uncertainty assessment; the paired interval pertains only to the\nimplemented capped summary and does not incorporate split variation or hyperparameter selection.\nWe do not pool checkpoint measurements into extra replications, infer confidence from overlapping\nseed markers, or interpret a zero-width interval as robustness across unseen settings. Reusing a seed\naligns the random-number initialization procedure, but differently shaped networks do not have\nidentical initial functions. Likewise, the two operations share an algebraic family while having\ndifferent fixed input partitions. Pairing improves the definition of a conditional comparison; it does\nnot turn those observations into independent draws from a broad population of models or tasks.\nConfiguration and evidence boundaries.\nThe final factorial evaluates one fixed learning-rate\nrecipe. The absence of a matched final result for the original lower-rate configuration precludes a\nfinal-baseline improvement claim. The recorded final evaluation completed and was accepted under\nits specified criteria. That acceptance certifies neither generalization nor a neural mechanism, and\nthe retained metadata does not provide a comprehensive integrity or adaptivity audit. Claims are\ntherefore tied to inspectable evaluator observations and readable implementation snapshots rather\nthan to acceptance labels or hashes alone.\n6\nCONCLUSION\nModel size can change the onset and attainability of training-set fitting without producing an ob-\nserved generalization transition.\nIn the evaluated modular addition and subtraction grid, wider\nfixed-rate ReLU networks memorize earlier without decay and overcome the narrowest models’\nfitting deficit with positive decay. Nevertheless, none of the 36 complete records reaches sustained\ngeneralization by 20,000 updates. The supported conclusion is a finite-horizon separation between\nfitting and held-out learning, not a claim that scale accelerates, prevents, or leaves unchanged even-\ntual grokking.\nThe distinction is consequential for measurement. An endpoint-substituted lag can display a nonzero\nwidth contrast with no seed variation even when its generalization event is never observed. Report-\ning event attainment, fitting times, and the observation boundary alongside that statistic is essential\nto its interpretation. A stronger account of width-dependent delayed generalization would need\nobserved transitions, more initialization and split replication, and controls separating width, initial-\n8\n\nPAGE 9\nization scale, and regularization. Those measurements are not supplied here. The present evidence\ninstead establishes which part of the scale question can be answered within the measured budget,\nand which part remains unidentified.\n9\n\nPAGE 10\nREFERENCES\nXander Davies, Lauro Langosco, and David Krueger. Unifying grokking and double descent. arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2303.06173, 2023. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.06173.\nTanishq Kumar, Blake Bordelon, Samuel J. Gershman, and Cengiz Pehlevan.\nGrokking as the\ntransition from lazy to rich training dynamics. arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.06110, 2023. URL\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2310.06110.\nZiming Liu, Eric J. Michaud, and Max Tegmark. Omnigrok: Grokking beyond algorithmic data.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2210.01117, 2022. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.01117.\nIlya Loshchilov and Frank Hutter.\nDecoupled weight decay regularization.\narXiv preprint\narXiv:1711.05101, 2017. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.05101.\nPreetum Nakkiran, Gal Kaplun, Yamini Bansal, Tristan Yang, Boaz Barak, and Ilya Sutskever. Deep\ndouble descent: Where bigger models and more data hurt. arXiv preprint arXiv:1912.02292,\n2019. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02292.\nAlethea Power, Yuri Burda, Harri Edwards, Igor Babuschkin, and Vedant Misra. Grokking: Gen-\neralization beyond overfitting on small algorithmic datasets. arXiv preprint arXiv:2201.02177,\n2022. URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.02177.\nVikrant Varma, Rohin Shah, Zachary Kenton, J´anos Kram´ar, and Ramana Kumar.\nExplaining\ngrokking through circuit efficiency.\narXiv preprint arXiv:2309.02390, 2023.\nURL https:\n//arxiv.org/abs/2309.02390.\nChiyuan Zhang, Samy Bengio, Moritz Hardt, Benjamin Recht, and Oriol Vinyals. Understanding\ndeep learning requires rethinking generalization. arXiv preprint arXiv:1611.03530, 2016. URL\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/1611.03530.\n10\n\nPAGE 11\nA\nSUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION\nA.1\nDATA CONSTRUCTION AND IMPLEMENTATION EVIDENCE\nThe source bundle contains the evaluator that owns training, the evaluated learning-rate controller,\nthe retained final data partition, dependencies, and final observation records. The relevant locations\nare listed below so that implementation claims can be checked directly, rather than inferred from\nprovenance hashes.\n• source evidence/evaluate final.py:588: canonical input encoding, normal-\nization, hash ordering, partition selection, and operation labels.\n• source evidence/evaluate final.py:721:\nsustained-event extraction and\nendpoint substitution; line 746 defines paired aggregation and its interval.\n• source evidence/evaluate final.py:789: deterministic training worker; line\n802 constructs features; line 828 defines the network; line 846 constructs AdamW; line 858\nbegins full-batch optimization.\n• source evidence/controller/src/program.py:46:\nreset, shared recipe,\nand schedule responses. The specification identifies the entrypoint within the adjacent\nsource directory.\nFor each operation, enumerate all ordered pairs and construct the JSON object with keys\noperation, p, a, and b in that order, using compact separators.\nApply NFKC normaliza-\ntion and whitespace canonicalization, then SHA-256.\nSort tuples of the digest and input in-\ntegers.\nThe final partition retains the portion starting at integer index ⌊p2/2⌋.\nReorder it by\nthe SHA-256 digest of the string train-v1: concatenated with the original hex digest. The\nfirst ⌊0.3n⌋entries train the model and the remainder are held out.\nThe stored resource is\nsource evidence/resources/final/benchmark.json. No external dataset, account,\nor license-controlled data is needed.\nThe evaluator file and observation-file hashes match their counterparts in the final metadata. These\nchecks establish consistency of the supplied artifacts, not the substantive correctness of every im-\nplementation claim. The readable source establishes the architecture and measurements. The final\nevidence registry retains an unverified diagnostics-trust designation; the paper does not upgrade that\ndesignation to an independent audit result.\nA.2\nCOMPLETE SEED-LEVEL OBSERVATIONS\nThe following table includes all completed conditions. Train and held-out accuracies average the\nfinal five checkpoints. Generalization is unobserved in every row; the LH column is therefore a\ncapped statistic, not an observed grokking time. All values are regenerated from retained final\nrecords by build artifacts.py. The main event table retains the evidence necessary for the\ncentral conclusion; this expanded view preserves seed variation and endpoint magnitudes.\nOp.\nλ\nWidth\nSeed\nTM\nLH\nTrain (%)\nHeld-out (%)\nadd.\n0\n16\n1009\n1500\n18500\n100.000\n0.474\nadd.\n0\n16\n2027\n1000\n19000\n100.000\n0.425\nadd.\n0\n16\n3037\n1000\n19000\n100.000\n0.546\nadd.\n0\n64\n1009\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.043\nadd.\n0\n64\n2027\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.079\nadd.\n0\n64\n3037\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.018\nadd.\n0\n256\n1009\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.000\nadd.\n0\n256\n2027\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.000\nadd.\n0\n256\n3037\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.000\nadd.\n1\n16\n1009\n—\n20000\n57.094\n0.200\nadd.\n1\n16\n2027\n—\n20000\n57.293\n0.249\nadd.\n1\n16\n3037\n—\n20000\n55.606\n0.206\nadd.\n1\n64\n1009\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.091\nadd.\n1\n64\n2027\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.079\nadd.\n1\n64\n3037\n500\n19500\n100.000\n0.103\n11\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
